        "id": "def-ip-noise-level-free-parameter-rule",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Noise-level-free parameter-choice rule",
        "statement": "Fix a compact Hilbert inverse model $K:X\\to Y$ and a family of reconstruction maps $(R_\\alpha)_{\\alpha>0}$, with each $R_\\alpha:Y\\to X$, both held fixed. A deterministic parameter-choice rule is a function $\\alpha:Y\\to(0,\\infty)$. It is **noise-level-free** (also called **heuristic**) when the value $\\alpha(z)$ for an observed datum $z\\in Y$ uses only $K$, the fixed family, and $z$; it does not take the exact datum or a noise bound $\\delta$ as input. Here $\\delta\\ge0$ is a valid noise bound for $z$ and an exact datum $y$ when $\\|z-y\\|_Y\\le\\delta$. The resulting reconstruction is $R_{\\alpha(z)}z$.",
        "deps": [
          "def-ip-compact-linear-inverse-model"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2
