      {
        "id": "thm-ip-bakushinskii-veto",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Bakushinskii veto for discontinuous pseudoinverses",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $K:X\\to Y$ be a compact linear operator between real or complex Hilbert spaces over one scalar field, with Hilbert-space Moore–Penrose inverse $K^\\dagger:D(K^\\dagger)\\to X$. Suppose $K^\\dagger$ is discontinuous on $D(K^\\dagger)$ with its norm inherited from $Y$. Let $(R_\\alpha)_{\\alpha>0}$ be maps $Y\\to X$ such that $R_\\alpha y\\to K^\\dagger y$ for every $y\\in D(K^\\dagger)$ as $\\alpha\\downarrow0$, and let $\\alpha:Y\\to(0,\\infty)$ be a deterministic noise-level-free rule. For each exact datum $y\\in D(K^\\dagger)$ and $\\delta>0$, set $E_y(\\delta):=\\sup\\{\\|R_{\\alpha(z)}z-K^\\dagger y\\|_X:z\\in Y,\\ \\|z-y\\|_Y\\le\\delta\\}$. If $E_y(\\delta)\\to0$ as $\\delta\\downarrow0$ for every $y\\in D(K^\\dagger)$, then $K^\\dagger$ is continuous on $D(K^\\dagger).$ Thus, when $K^\\dagger$ is discontinuous, no such noise-level-free rule yields worst-case convergence over each exact datum's full admissible error balls. The limit is required separately for each exact datum, not uniformly over $y$. This veto does not exclude convergence on restricted solution or error classes, stochastic noise models, or finite-dimensional problems.",
